---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { BUSINESS } from '../config/site';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---

<Legal
  title="Feedback and complaints policy"
  metaTitle="Feedback and complaints policy | Espoir Care"
  description="How to give feedback or make a complaint to Espoir Care, how we handle it under the NDIS Practice Standards and who else you can contact."
  path="/complaints-and-feedback/"
  lead="We want to hear what we are doing well and what we could do better. This policy explains how to tell us and what we do next."
>
  <h2>Your feedback matters</h2>
  <p>
    Compliments, ideas and complaints all help us improve. Making a complaint will never affect the support you receive from us. You can stay anonymous
    if you prefer, although that can make it harder for us to look into things.
  </p>

  <h2>About this policy</h2>
  <p>
    This policy explains how Espoir Care welcomes, records and resolves feedback and complaints. It follows the NDIS Practice Standards and the National
    Disability Insurance Scheme (Complaints Management and Resolution) Rules 2018.
  </p>
  <p>It applies to everyone we support and their families, carers and advocates. It also applies to all our staff.</p>

  <h2>Our promises to you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It is safe to speak up.</strong> Nobody will be treated differently or lose any support because they gave feedback or made a complaint.</li>
    <li><strong>We are fair.</strong> We listen to everyone involved and give them a chance to tell their side before we decide anything.</li>
    <li><strong>We keep it private.</strong> We only share details with the people who need them to sort out the complaint, or when the law says we must.</li>
    <li><strong>We make it easy.</strong> You can tell us in whatever way suits you, in your own words.</li>
    <li><strong>We are honest.</strong> If we got something wrong, we say so, say sorry and put it right where we can.</li>
    <li><strong>We learn.</strong> Every complaint helps us improve how we support people.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who can give feedback or make a complaint</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone can. That includes the people we support, family members, carers, guardians, advocates, support coordinators, other providers, our own staff
    and members of the public.
  </p>

  <h2>How to tell us</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Talk to your support worker or nurse</li>
    <li>Call us on <a href={BUSINESS.phoneHref}>{BUSINESS.phone}</a></li>
    <li>Email <a href={`mailto:${BUSINESS.email}`}>{BUSINESS.email}</a></li>
    <li>Send a message through our <a href={url('/contact/')}>contact page</a></li>
  </ul>
  <p>You do not need to put it in writing. If you tell us in person or over the phone, we will write it down for you and check we have it right.</p>

  <h2>Getting help to make a complaint</h2>
  <p>
    A family member, friend or advocate can make a complaint for you or help you with it. To find a free disability advocate, visit the
    <a href="https://disabilityadvocacyfinder.dss.gov.au/" rel="noopener">Disability Advocacy Finder</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you need an interpreter to contact the NDIS Quality and Safeguards Commission, call TIS National on 131 450. If you are deaf or find it hard to
    hear or speak, you can use the <a href="https://www.accesshub.gov.au/about-the-nrs" rel="noopener">National Relay Service</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens when you make a complaint</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>We listen and record it.</strong> We write down what happened and what you would like to happen.</li>
    <li><strong>We let you know we have it within 24 hours.</strong> We tell you who is looking after your complaint and how to reach them.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>We keep people safe first.</strong> If anyone is at risk, we act straight away. If the complaint is about a reportable incident, we tell the
      NDIS Quality and Safeguards Commission as the law requires.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>We look into it.</strong> We talk with you, the staff involved and anyone else who can help, and we check our records. Staff involved get a
      fair chance to respond. The person looking into it is never someone the complaint is about.
    </li>
    <li><strong>We work out a fix with you.</strong> We ask what outcome you are hoping for and talk through the options.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>We aim to resolve it within 24 to 48 hours.</strong> Every complaint is different. Some take longer depending on what happened. If yours
      will take longer, we tell you why and keep you updated.
    </li>
    <li><strong>We tell you the outcome.</strong> We explain what we found and what we will do, in the way you prefer.</li>
    <li><strong>We check you are happy with it.</strong> If you are not, you can ask for a review.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>If you are not happy with the outcome</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask for your complaint to be reviewed by a senior person in our team who was not involved in the first decision. You can also go to any of the
    organisations below at any time.
  </p>

  <h3>NDIS participants</h3>
  <p>You can contact the NDIS Quality and Safeguards Commission at any time. You do not have to come to us first.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Phone: 1800 035 544 (free call from landlines)</li>
    <li>Website: <a href="https://www.ndiscommission.gov.au/" rel="noopener">ndiscommission.gov.au</a>, where you can also make a complaint online</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>DVA clients</h3>
  <p>You can give feedback to the Department of Veterans' Affairs.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Phone: 1800 VETERAN (1800 838 372)</li>
    <li>Website: <a href="https://www.dva.gov.au/" rel="noopener">dva.gov.au</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Complaints about nursing or health care</h3>
  <p>You can contact the Office of the Health Ombudsman in Queensland.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Phone: 133 OHO (133 646)</li>
    <li>Website: <a href="https://www.oho.qld.gov.au/" rel="noopener">oho.qld.gov.au</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Privacy complaints</h3>
  <p>See the complaints section of our <a href={url('/privacy-policy/')}>privacy policy</a>.</p>

  <h2>How we keep records</h2>
  <p>
    We record every complaint, what we did about it and the outcome in our complaints register. We store these records securely and keep them for 7
    years, as the NDIS rules require.
  </p>

  <h2>How we learn from complaints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We review the complaints register regularly to spot patterns and fix the cause, not just the single problem.</li>
    <li>We talk about what we have learned, without naming anyone, at our monthly team meetings and training.</li>
    <li>We review this policy at least once a year, and sooner if the rules change.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What our staff do</h2>
  <p>
    Every staff member learns this policy at induction and in our ongoing training. Staff help anyone who wants to give feedback or make a complaint,
    and pass it on to the right person straight away. Staff can raise concerns of their own too, without fear of being treated badly.
  </p>

  <h2>If someone is in danger</h2>
  <p>
    If someone is in immediate danger, call 000. If you are worried about abuse or neglect of a person with disability, you can also report it to the NDIS
    Quality and Safeguards Commission on 1800 035 544.
  </p>
</Legal>
